metal2vulkan: framebuffer fetch on a device with neither ordered form, through unordered input attachments (Scion)

Upstream:  metal2vulkan, https://github.com/steelbrain/metal2vulkan, at 720ef1ce9ae2a2068999a8675717418b9416283e, after
           AdrenoMetal's 16 patches to it and Scion's 0001 to 0004; tools/metal/mk-metal2vulkan.sh applies them in that order,
           then this.
Licence:   GPL-2.0-or-later OR LGPL-3.0-or-later, metal2vulkan's (its LICENSE); this patch is a change to it and under the same
           terms. Nothing of it is in Scion's MIT or APSL files.
Author:    the HakiOS authors (Scion), 14 November 2026.

Why. CoreAnimation's window server (backboardd, camtl=1) reads its colour attachment in luma_boost_lph ([[color(0)]],
framebuffer fetch), and programs written for Apple's GPUs assume the fetch. metal2vulkan's fetch is exact through
rasterization-order input attachments or the pixel interlock; PanVK v6 (the Galaxy S9's Mali-G72) has neither, so the
pipeline was refused Unsupported(FramebufferFetch) and QuartzCore aborted ("Metal failed to build render pipeline:
spec=PBGRAXm_TlubA2Xhf_Isrc"). The compile layer already reads such a fetch through input attachments without that order under
FramebufferFetchPolicy::AcceptUnordered (PrecisionNote::UnorderedFramebufferFetch); the runtime refused every such pipeline.

What.
- DeviceProfile::framebuffer_fetch(): the policy, public, for the runtime.
- framebuffer_fetch::unordered_inputs: a device with neither rasterizationOrderColorAttachmentAccess nor the pixel interlock
  whose profile accepts the unordered form. There every pass reads its colour attachments as input attachments, as on a device
  with the ordered access (through_inputs: render targets take INPUT_ATTACHMENT usage, image_plan.rs; the subpass references
  them, render_target.rs), without the rasterization-order flags on the subpass and the pipelines (orders_inputs). The runtime
  makes the compile layer's unordered pipelines there (ordered() accepts them); elsewhere it still refuses them.
- Between draws: the draws' subpass already depends on itself, by region, from colour attachment writes to input attachment
  reads (DRAWS_SELF_DEPENDENCY). A draw that reads input attachments without rasterization order records a barrier of that
  whole dependency first wherever an earlier draw of its render pass instance wrote a colour since the last such barrier
  (render.rs: InterlockedStorage::attachments, DrawBarrier; the pending draw's barrier too, render/pending.rs). So a fetch
  reads every earlier draw's value at its pixel, as Metal's does. A pass's first instance whose colour loads by a clear counts
  the clear as such a write: PanVK reads an input attachment through memory, which the clear reaches only when a barrier ends
  the tile's work (with Scion's tools/mesa/panvk/panvk-barrier-clears.patch, which keeps the clear at such a barrier).
- Within one draw the fetch stays unordered: a fragment may read its pixel as it was before the same draw's earlier fragments
  there. A draw that reads each pixel once (a full-screen pass, CoreAnimation's luma boost) reads Metal's value. The compile
  layer's precision note announces it once.
- A BGR10_XR attachment that renders through the unorm view of its word (0004) is not read so: the input attachment would read
  the view's codes, not the raw texel the read decodes. Refused NotImplemented(FramebufferFetch) at that attachment.
- ProgrammableBlending (the family feature) stays refused without rasterization-order access.

The policy is the caller's: Scion's bridge (tools/metal/bridge, nmb_device_open) passes AcceptUnordered. Under the default
RequireOrdered nothing changes.

Checked on the phone (PanVK on the Mali-G72): metal_tri --fetch (tools/metal/air/tri_fetch.ll, the corners' mix over 2 plus
[[color(0)]] over 2, drawn twice into a cleared BGRA8 target, a texture and a texture over an IOSurface): every pixel inside
within 0.9/255 of 3/4 mix + 1/4 clear, every pixel outside the clear exactly, at 256 and 1024 pixels square, five runs of five.
Before PanVK's barrier fix the first draw read zeros instead of the clear; before the barrier the pipeline was refused.
metal_tri plain and --xr none, over, add pass as before.

